        <!-- SECTION 4 -->
        <h2 id="subscriptions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Subscriptions and Ongoing Costs to Budget For
        </h2>
        <p>
          A treadmill's sticker price isn't always the full cost of
          ownership, and this bracket is exactly where that distinction
          starts to matter. Machines from NordicTrack and ProForm in this
          price range are built around the iFit platform, and while the
          hardware works fine without a subscription, in the sense that
          you can still walk or run manually, most of what makes the
          touchscreen interface worthwhile, trainer-led workouts,
          automatic incline adjustment tied to scenery, and structured
          training plans, sits behind a paid membership running roughly
          <strong>$39 a month</strong> after an included trial period.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sole and Horizon, by contrast, generally sell their machines in
          this bracket without any subscription requirement for core
          functionality. You lose the trainer-led video content and
          automatic terrain-matching, but you gain a lower total cost of
          ownership and no ongoing bill to track. Neither approach is
          wrong, but it's worth being honest with yourself about whether
          you'll actually use guided workouts regularly before paying a
          premium for touchscreen hardware that assumes you will.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond subscriptions, budget for a few accessories that make a
          real difference at this price point: a proper equipment mat
          (<strong>$30-$60</strong>) to reduce noise and protect flooring,
          and if the machine doesn't include one, a Bluetooth chest strap
          heart rate monitor (<strong>$40-$80</strong>) for more accurate
          tracking than wrist-based sensors typically provide during
          treadmill workouts specifically.
        </p>
        <p>
          Delivery and assembly are two more line items worth planning
          for, even though they're easy to overlook when comparing sticker
          prices. Most machines in this bracket ship via freight or a
          large parcel carrier and weigh well over 200 pounds fully
          assembled, so curbside delivery is common unless you pay extra
          for white-glove service, typically an additional
          <strong>$100-$150</strong>. Self-assembly generally takes
          60-90 minutes with two people and a set of basic tools, longer
          if you're installing the console and running cables for the
          first time. If you're not confident tackling that yourself,
          factor white-glove delivery into your total budget comparison
          rather than treating it as a hidden extra after the fact.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's also worth thinking about long-term maintenance costs,
          which tend to be modest but not zero. Budget roughly
          <strong>$50-$100 a year</strong> for belt lubrication supplies,
          and expect to eventually replace a running belt after several
          years of heavy use, typically another <strong>$80-$150</strong>
          plus installation if you don't do it yourself. None of this
          should be a dealbreaker at this price point, but it's worth
          knowing upfront rather than being surprised a few years in.
        </p>

