        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <h2 id="when-to-stretch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          When It's Worth Stretching Your Budget
        </h2>
        <p>
          Every bracket has an edge case, and this one is no exception.
          If your top priority is incline training, interval variety, or
          you specifically want the largest touchscreen and most premium
          feel available without moving into full commercial territory, a
          few hundred dollars above $1,500 buys a meaningfully different
          experience, as our stretch pick above illustrates.
        </p>
        <p>
          That said, we'd caution against stretching your budget just to
          chase a slightly larger touchscreen or a nicer-looking frame if
          the underlying use case doesn't call for it. The machines
          firmly within this bracket cover the vast majority of home
          fitness needs, from steady cardio to structured interval
          training, and the returns on spending significantly more start
          to diminish unless you have a specific reason, like frequent
          heavy running, incline-focused training, or multiple household
          members with very different fitness levels sharing one machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          A useful gut check: if you can name the specific feature you'd
          be paying extra for, and you know you'll use it regularly,
          stretching the budget usually pays off. If you're stretching
          because a pricier machine simply looks nicer in photos, that's
          a weaker reason, and you're likely to be just as happy, and
          several hundred dollars richer, sticking with one of the picks
          firmly inside this bracket.
        </p>
        <p>
          There's also a timing element worth factoring in. Treadmill
          pricing fluctuates meaningfully around a handful of predictable
          sales windows each year, particularly late December through
          early January and again around major shopping holidays in
          spring and fall. A machine that lists at $1,799 in June has, in
          our experience tracking pricing across this category, a real
          chance of dropping into the low $1,600s or even high $1,500s
          during one of those windows. If you're on the fence about
          stretching your budget for a specific machine, waiting a few
          weeks for a known sales event is often a better strategy than
          either paying full price immediately or ruling the machine out
          entirely based on its non-sale price.
        </p>
        <p>
          One more consideration when deciding whether to stretch: resale
          value. Machines with stronger brand reputations and wider
          incline ranges, the kind you find just above this bracket,
          tend to hold value better in the used marketplace if your needs
          change or you relocate in a few years. That's not a reason to
          overspend on features you won't use, but it's a small factor
          worth weighing if you're genuinely torn between two options at
          different price points and expect to sell or pass along the
          machine eventually rather than running it into the ground.
        </p>

