              line in this guide are still strong choices.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do treadmills in this price range require a subscription?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It depends on the brand. NordicTrack and ProForm machines in
              this bracket are iFit-compatible and unlock most of their
              trainer-led content through a paid subscription, typically
              around <strong>$39/month</strong> after a trial. Sole and
              Horizon machines in this range generally do not require any
              subscription for core functionality, which is worth weighing
              against the interactive features you'd give up.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              What's the single most important spec to check under $1,500?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Weight capacity relative to your own body weight, with a
              buffer of at least <strong>20-30 pounds</strong>. Motor size
              and deck length matter too, but weight capacity is the one
              spec that, if too close to your own weight, can shorten the
              machine's lifespan regardless of how good the rest of the
              specs look on paper.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Should I consider a folding treadmill in this price bracket?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Most machines in this price range do fold, and folding
              capability doesn't typically come at a meaningful cost to
              build quality at this tier the way it can on ultra-budget
              models. If floor space is a concern, folding is a reasonable
              expectation to have even as you move up from entry-level
              pricing.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              If $1,500 is still too much, where should I look instead?
            </h3>
